  "verdict": "ARTIFACT-SOFTEN",
  "verdict_text": "The near/far RATIO is NON-DISCRIMINATING: in-distribution airfoils with GOOD predictions (rel-L2 0.006) show a near/far PDE-only ratio (23.2) that is AS LARGE AS the cylinder's (18.5; cyl/airfoil=0.80x). Near-body residual concentration is therefore a GENERAL near-wall phenomenon, not a failure localizer -- the cylinder ratio (14.6/22.9/21.0) cannot be cited as evidence the residual 'localizes the OOD failure'. HOWEVER, the ABSOLUTE residual magnitude DOES discriminate: the OOD cylinder is ~6.4x higher near-body (2.18 vs 0.338) AND ~7.4x higher in the FAR FIELD (0.122 vs 0.0166) -- the far field is away from any wall, so its gap CANNOT be a stencil artifact. RECOMMENDATION: drop the near/far-ratio framing; keep the OOD-detection thesis but pin it to the ABSOLUTE residual magnitude (field-wide, far-field included): the field is uniformly less PDE-consistent on the novel geometry, most so on its boundary."
